---
layout: article
title: Bots and AI traffic
description: How Appwrite Analytics recognizes bots, AI crawlers, and AI assistants from their user agent, where they appear on the dashboard, and how they count toward your plan.
---

Not every visit comes from a person. Search engines index your pages, AI companies crawl them for training and answers, social sites fetch link previews, and monitors check that your site is up. Appwrite recognizes these clients from their user agent and labels their events, so you can see how much of your traffic is automated and who sends it.

# How bots are recognized {% #detection %}

Appwrite checks the user agent of every event: the browser's `User-Agent` header, or the `userAgent` your server or proxy passes on. When it matches a known bot, Appwrite records the event as bot traffic, with the bot's name and category.

Bots reach your analytics when they run your tracking code, such as crawlers that render JavaScript and automated browsers. They also appear when your server or proxy sends events for their requests with their user agent.

Generic HTTP clients, such as `curl` or a language's default HTTP library, aren't counted as bots.

# Categories {% #categories %}

| Category | Examples |
| --- | --- |
| AI crawler | GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, OAI SearchBot, CCBot, Google Extended, Bytespider, Amazonbot |
| AI assistant | ChatGPT User, Claude User |
| Search crawler | Googlebot, Bingbot, Applebot |
| Social preview | Facebook External Hit, Twitterbot, Slackbot |
| Site crawler | AhrefsBot, SemrushBot |
| Site monitor | UptimeRobot |
| Advertising crawler | Google AdsBot |
| Automation | Headless Chrome |
| Crawler | Any other client that calls itself a bot, crawler, spider, or scraper in its user agent |

**AI crawlers** collect pages to train models and build search indexes. **AI assistants** fetch a page while answering a person's question. People who click a link from an assistant to your site aren't bots: they count as visitors in the `ai` channel. See [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources#ai).

The API uses lowercase category names, such as `ai crawler` and `search crawler`.

# On the dashboard {% #dashboard %}

Two cards show automated traffic.

- **Humans vs bots** splits traffic into people and bots, by visitors or by events. Below the split, **By type** ranks bot categories and **Top agents** ranks individual bots.
- **AI agents and crawlers** lists every named bot in the date range on the **Agents** tab, and their categories on the **Categories** tab.

Click a category or a bot to filter the whole dashboard to it, so you can see which pages it reads and how often. You can also filter by **Bot** or **Bot category** from **Filters**.

The metrics at the top of the dashboard, such as **Unique visitors** and **Pageviews**, include bot traffic.

# Billing {% #billing %}

Bot events don't count toward your plan's analytics events. See [Limits and retention](/docs/products/analytics/limits).

# Block bots {% #block %}

Analytics shows bot traffic. To stop it from reaching a site, use [Firewall](/docs/products/firewall), for example with a rule that [challenges automated traffic](/docs/products/firewall/challenge-bots). From the dashboard, you can start a Firewall rule from any bot name. See [Block traffic with Firewall](/docs/products/analytics/firewall).
